{
  "case_id": "2016-16-348",
  "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0000-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We will hear argument next in Case 16-348, Midland Funding v. Johnson. Mr. Shanmugam."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: I'm sorry. What do you do with the language of Pennsylvania Public Welfare v. Davenport where we explicitly said that a claim is a right to payment and enforceable obligation? What do you do with that language?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Shanmugam",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I don't think that -- that that even rises to the level of, in the parlance of the last argument, a drive-by holding. And that's for the simple reason that neither Davenport nor the later cases citing Davenport in any way purported to somehow exclude unenforceable rights from the definition of a claim."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: So it's a breach of the trustee's duties if he or she lets the claim go through without objecting?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Shanmugam",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The trustee should object where, in the language of the statute, some purpose would be served."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: -- automatically object to anything that is over the -- the statute of limitations."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Geyser",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Our theory, I think, is exactly what Justice Sotomayor said earlier in the argument. A debt collector has to have a good faith belief that they have a right to payment under the code and have a valid and enforceable debt."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Geyser",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0001-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "satisfy and discharge the obligation they have to satisfy and discharge under Rule 9011. It's a basic --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0001-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Are there any affirmative defenses that your argument might apply to that are bankruptcy-related particularly? Or are these all kind of the sort of defenses that are involved in any suit?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Geyser",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think that in the bankruptcy setting, first of all, given the speed of the proof of claim process, the odds are the objection will be adjudicated before the FDCPA suit is -- is far underway or underway at all, which is actually what happened in this case. I also think that in most circumstances, the state of mind can be satisfied by the creditor by simply articulating any reason they filed the -- the suit. They simply have to explain, why did you -- why did you think you were entitled to collect? Because every claim in the bankruptcy process is automatically deemed prima facie valid and enforceable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "May I ask you the question that I -- I asked your -- your adversary. Why -- why do these time-barred claims slip through? I mean, that's a big part of your argument. Why don't trustees and attorneys for the debtor automatically object to any claim that is beyond the number of years set out in the statute of limitations?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Geyser",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think there are two reasons that they don't. The first is that the cost of objecting is sometimes more than the benefit of excluding the claim. These are nuisance-value claims. They often will acquiesce in a legitimate payout simply to avoid the nuisance value of the objection, which I think is unfair."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But how many of these would there be in the typical Chapter 13?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Geyser",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What -- what we've seen in at least the cases that have had a chance to go past the pleading stage is that the trustee, for example, the Middle District of Alabama processes between 6 and 7,000 claims a month. So to review the claims, they have to review a claim every two minutes for 365 days a year --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Geyser",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And the other reason that the trustees don't always object, Justice Alito, and they've told us this -- the -- the Chapter 13 trustees at page 15 of their brief, there -- there's an information asymmetry. Trustees assume that creditors act in good faith. So they assume if there's a facially time-barred claim, it's possible the creditor is aware of some basis for tolling. And the trustee doesn't know what the creditor is thinking, and so they might not object, which is a way that these claims are, in fact, misleading, even to sophisticated trustees."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In this case, is it -- is there something in the record? I mean, it's rather surprising to me that there is a company and their business model, you say, is to go around buying up debts that can't be enforced and are worthless, and then filing cases hoping that no one will notice. Is that shown in the record? I mean, is somebody -- they admit that's their business model? Where does this come from?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Geyser",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0002-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your Honor, I think it comes from -- first, this was dismissed on the pleadings, so we can develop it in the case. But it comes from the FTC report that analyzed the data of debt collectors and --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Sarah E. Harrington",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0002-0008",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF SARAH E. HARRINGTON FOR UNITED STATES, AS AMICUS CURIAE, SUPPORTING THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Sarah E. Harrington",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0002-0009",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In our view, no creditor is entitled to file a proof of claim in bankruptcy on a claim that the creditor knows is time barred. When the bankruptcy system works --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Harrington",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. HARRINGTON: -- I -- I think the one thing everyone agrees on in this case is that if the bankruptcy system works as Congress intended, 100 percent of time-barred claims will be disallowed. That is what Congress intended, but --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Harrington",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. HARRINGTON: Yes. In our view these are claims within the meaning of the Bankruptcy Code."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Is your argument --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Shanmugam",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Just two points on rebuttal. First, I think, with all due respect, the Court should be concerned about the breadth of Respondent's position, and let me lay out circumstances that I think would be covered by Respondent's rule."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2016-16-348",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "33dbd2c03434884269ec64ed27a94cccd1150d1dcc8ccaaa54473592386cbbf4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-33dbd2c03434-0003-0005",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
    {
